The list is reversed by changing one `next` pointer at a time. `previous` stores the part that is already reversed, while `current` moves through the old list.

The old next node must be saved before changing `current->next`. This also works for an empty list and a list with only one node.

```cpp
/*
Definition of singly linked list:
struct ListNode
{
    int val;
    ListNode *next;
    ListNode()
    {
        val = 0;
        next = NULL;
    }
    ListNode(int data1)
    {
        val = data1;
        next = NULL;
    }
    ListNode(int data1, ListNode *next1)
    {
        val = data1;
        next = next1;
    }
};
*/

class Solution {
public:
    ListNode* reverseList(ListNode* head) {
        ListNode* previous = NULL;
        ListNode* current = head;

        while (current != NULL) {
            ListNode* nextNode = current->next;
            current->next = previous;
            previous = current;
            current = nextNode;
        }

        return previous;
    }
};
```

For `1 -> 2 -> 3`, the first pass makes `1 -> NULL`, the second makes `2 -> 1`, and the last makes `3 -> 2 -> 1`.

Time complexity: `O(n)`  
Space complexity: `O(1)`

The input list is changed in place.

A recursive call goes to the end of the list first. When it returns, the current node is placed after its next node, and its old forward link is removed.

The base case covers both `null` and the last node. The recursive version is short, but it uses the call stack, so a very long list can use more memory.

```java
/*Definition of singly linked list:
class ListNode {
    int val;
    ListNode next;

    ListNode() {
        val = 0;
        next = null;
    }

    ListNode(int data1) {
        val = data1;
        next = null;
    }

    ListNode(int data1, ListNode next1) {
        val = data1;
        next = next1;
    }
}
 */

class Solution {
    public ListNode reverseList(ListNode head) {
        if (head == null || head.next == null) {
            return head;
        }

        ListNode newHead = reverseList(head.next);
        head.next.next = head;
        head.next = null;

        return newHead;
    }
}
```

The returned node is the original tail. If the input is empty, the method simply returns `null`.

Time complexity: `O(n)`  
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the recursive call stack

The links in the original list are reversed, rather than creating new nodes.

The recursive call keeps moving until it reaches the last node. While the calls return, each node is attached behind its next node. Setting `head.next` to `null` prevents an unwanted cycle.

This keeps the code small, though the recursion depth grows with the list length.

```javascript
/*Definition of singly linked list:
class ListNode {
    constructor(val = 0, next = null) {
        this.val = val;
        this.next = next;
    }
}
*/

class Solution {
    reverseList(head) {
        if (head === null || head.next === null) {
            return head;
        }

        const newHead = this.reverseList(head.next);
        head.next.next = head;
        head.next = null;

        return newHead;
    }
}
```

For a one-node list, the node is already reversed. For an empty list, the result is also `null`, so neither case needs special list creation.

Time complexity: `O(n)`  
Space complexity: `O(n)` because of recursive calls

This modifies the original linked list in place.

## Solution
```javascript[]
/*Definition of singly linked list:
class ListNode {
    constructor(val = 0, next = null) {
        this.val = val;
        this.next = next;
    }
}
*/

class Solution {
  reverseList(head) {
    if (head === null || head.next === null) {
      return head;
    }
    let newHead = this.reverseList(head.next);
    let store = head.next;
    head.next = null;
    store.next = head;
    return newHead;
  }
}

